+# Hegel property-based testing report: `polyline` 0.11.0
+
+Target: georust/polyline (Google Encoded Polyline encoder/decoder), commit `7fc654d`.
+Tests added to the existing `tests` module in `src/lib.rs` (10 properties, `hegeltest` 0.28.2).
+Note: the run prompt mentioned existing quickcheck tests, but this checkout has none —
+existing coverage is example-based unit tests in `src/lib.rs` plus a seeded-rand criterion
+bench. The unit tests were kept unchanged and supplemented.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| Test | Property | Evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| `prop_encode_decode_roundtrip_within_tolerance` | Crown roundtrip: for any line of in-range coords (incl. empty, single-point, up to 200 points), `decode(encode(line, p), p)` recovers every coordinate within `0.5 * 10^-p + 1e-10`, per coordinate (not cumulative — delta encoding drift would fail this), at p = 5 and 6. Also `encoded.is_empty() == line.is_empty()` and length preservation. | Crate docs: "lossy compression algorithm"; `scale()` rounds to the 10^-p grid; existing `precision5`/`precision6`/`rounding_error` unit tests. |
+| `prop_higher_precision_loses_less` | Precision monotonicity: per coordinate, the p=6 roundtrip error is ≤ the p=5 error (+1e-9 for f64 tie noise). Grounded in the 1e-5 grid being a subset of the 1e-6 grid. | Algorithm definition (grid rounding at 10^-p). |
+| `prop_decode_encode_fixpoint` | Canonical roundtrip: `encode(decode(s, p), p) == s` for any `s` produced by the encoder, and decoding again is a value fixpoint. Precision drawn 0..=9. | Decoded values sit exactly on the grid (integer deltas accumulated in i64, divided once); existing unit tests assert exact string equality both ways. |
+| `prop_rounding_boundary_roundtrip` | Boundary hunting: coordinates constructed exactly on grid points `k*10^-p` and at round-half tie offsets (±0.49/±0.5/±0.51 grid steps), clamped to ±90/±180, still encode Ok and decode within tolerance. | `scale()` uses `.round()` (half-away-from-zero); existing `limits` and `rounding_error` tests. |
+| `prop_out_of_range_coordinates_rejected` | Validation: inserting one out-of-range coordinate (beyond ±90/±180, ±inf, or NaN) at any position in an otherwise-valid line makes `encode_coordinates` return the matching error variant with the correct `idx` and offending `coord` value. | `MIN/MAX_LATITUDE/LONGITUDE` range checks in `encode_coordinates`; `PolylineError::{Latitude,Longitude}CoordError` fields; existing `bad_coords` test. |
+| `prop_decode_never_panics_on_arbitrary_strings` | Parse robustness: `decode_polyline` on arbitrary full-Unicode strings (precision 0..=9) returns Ok/Err, never panics. | Doc: "Returns an error if the polyline is invalid"; existing `broken_string`/`invalid_string` tests. |
+| `prop_decode_polyline_alphabet_no_panic_and_in_range` | Parse robustness on polyline-shaped input: strings of bytes 63..=126 (the polyline alphabet, up to ~300 bytes to force long varint chains) never panic, and any `Ok` result contains only in-range coordinates. | Doc: "Returns an error … if the decoded coordinates are out of bounds"; the `shift > 64 - 5` overflow guard in `decode_next`; existing `should_not_trigger_overflow` test. |
+| `prop_decode_corrupted_polyline_never_panics` | Corruption robustness: truncating a valid polyline at any byte, overwriting any byte with arbitrary ASCII, or inserting an ASCII byte is handled gracefully (no panic; Ok results in range). | Existing `truncated` test (one hardcoded case) generalized. |
+| `prop_empty_and_single_point_roundtrip` | Degenerate shapes: empty line ↔ `""` exactly; single-point line roundtrips within tolerance. | `encode_coordinates` loop structure (empty iterator → empty string); decode `while peek` loop. |
+| `prop_any_precision_no_panic` | The `precision: u32` parameter is unrestricted and undocumented-unbounded; encode/decode should return Ok/Err for any value, never panic. **FAILS — real bug, see below.** | Public signatures `encode_coordinates(_, precision: u32)` / `decode_polyline(_, precision: u32)`; no documented bound. |
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test`: **19 passed, 1 failed** — the failure is `prop_any_precision_no_panic`,
+kept failing deliberately to pin a real bug (see below). All 10 pre-existing unit
+tests still pass.
+
+Run-and-reflect checks performed:
+- **Liveness**: temporarily set the crown-roundtrip tolerance to 0 — hegel failed with
+  a shrunk counterexample (`lat 1.015625 -> 1.01563, precision 5`); reverted, `.hegel/` cleared.
+- **Guarded branches fire**: with temporary counters at 10x cases, the `if let Ok(...)`
+  branch fired 91/1000 cases in the alphabet test and 197/1000 in the corruption test —
+  neither property is vacuous.
+- **Exploratory 10x run**: all properties run at `test_cases = 1000` (verified via a case
+  counter printing 1003 `RT_CASE` lines, not wall clock). No additional failures found;
+  instrumentation reverted (final file diff-identical to the reviewed version plus nothing).
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**Panic (debug) / silent wrap (release) on `precision >= 10` in both `encode_coordinates`
+and `decode_polyline`.**
+
+- Minimal counterexample (hegel-shrunk, reproduces every run): `precision = 10` with any
+  non-empty input, e.g. `encode_coordinates(vec![Coord { x: -120.2, y: 38.5 }], 10)`.
+- Failure: `attempt to multiply with overflow` in `i32::pow` (thread panic, debug profile).
+- Code path: `src/lib.rs:75` (`let factor: i32 = base.pow(precision);` in
+  `encode_coordinates`) and `src/lib.rs:131` (`i64::from(base.pow(precision))` in
+  `decode_polyline`). `10^10 = 10_000_000_000 > i32::MAX`; in release builds `pow`
+  wraps instead, producing a garbage factor and silently wrong coordinates rather than
+  an `Err`.
+- Why a library bug, not a test bug: oracle-independent (a panic), on a public API whose
+  `precision: u32` parameter documents no upper bound, in a crate whose decode API
+  explicitly promises `Err` on invalid input. Either the overflow should return
+  `Err`, or the valid precision range should be documented/enforced.
+- Pinned by `prop_any_precision_no_panic` (KNOWN FAILURE comment in the test); the
+  generator includes a `sampled_from([10, 11, 31, u32::MAX])` branch so the failure is
+  deterministic, and it shrinks to `precision = 10`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+None abandoned outright. Two properties were designed around subtleties rather than
+discovered by failure:
+- Precision monotonicity is asserted per-coordinate with a `+1e-9` epsilon because
+  `.round()` half-away-from-zero ties plus f64 product rounding in `scale()` can make
+  the p=6 error equal (not strictly less than) the p=5 error.
+- The canonical decode→encode fixpoint is only claimed for encoder-produced strings
+  (encode∘decode∘encode == encode), not for arbitrary valid-alphabet strings — a
+  hand-crafted polyline may use non-canonical chunk encodings the encoder never emits,
+  and the crate makes no canonicalization promise.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Mostly everything just worked; hegeltest 0.28.2 compiled and ran first try on this
+edition-2021 crate.
+
+- The rust reference's gotcha list was accurate and useful: the `Generator` trait
+  import warning (gotcha in reference.md line ~183) hit exactly as described — I
+  imported `Generator` preemptively, used only builder/config methods and `one_of!`,
+  and got `unused_imports`; the reference told me in advance this would happen but it
+  would be worth stating explicitly that `one_of!` does *not* require the trait import.
+- The "helper functions taking `&TestCase`" pattern was the right tool for
+  `draw_lat`/`draw_lng`/`draw_line`; clearer than composites for this crate. Reference
+  section was clear.
+- The SKILL.md guidance "confirm the guarded branch is reached" (Run and Reflect) was
+  directly load-bearing here: two properties have `if let Ok(...)` guards, and the
+  counter check proved they fire (9%/20% of cases). Without that instruction the
+  alphabet property could plausibly have been vacuous.
+- The "no global case-count override" gotcha (no `PROPTEST_CASES` equivalent) cost a
+  sed round-trip to run the 10x pass and revert. A `HEGEL_TEST_CASES` env var would
+  remove the most error-prone manual step in the workflow.
+- SKILL.md's "Generate Configurations, Not Just Payloads" is what prompted drawing
+  `precision` as an input rather than hardcoding 5 — that is what found the only real
+  bug. Worth cross-linking that section from the roundtrip pattern, since codecs'
+  precision/level parameters are exactly this shape.
